    <h2>LLM Architecture</h2>

    <h3>Decoder-Only Transformers</h3>
    
    <p>Most modern LLMs (GPT, Llama, Claude) use decoder-only architecture:</p>

    <pre><code>Architecture Stack (e.g., GPT-4):
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│ Token + Position Embeddings     │
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│ Transformer Layer 1              │
│   - Multi-Head Attention         │
│   - Feed-Forward Network         │
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│ Transformer Layer 2              │
│   ...                            │
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│ Transformer Layer N (96+ layers) │
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│ Layer Normalization              │
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│ Output Projection (vocab_size)   │
└─────────────────────────────────┘

Typical specs (GPT-3 175B):
- Layers: 96
- Hidden size: 12,288
- Attention heads: 96

Context Windows (2024 State-of-the-art):
- GPT-4 Turbo: 128K tokens (~96K words)
- GPT-4o: 128K tokens
- Claude 3 Opus/Sonnet: 200K tokens (~150K words)
- Llama 3.1: 128K tokens
- Gemini 1.5 Pro: 1M tokens (~750K words)
- Gemini 1.5 Flash: 1M tokens
- Gemini 2.0 Flash: 1M tokens (up to 2M in testing)

Long context enables:
• Processing entire codebases (10K+ files)
• Analyzing full books or research papers
• Multi-hour conversation history
• Complex document understanding
• "Needle in haystack" retrieval across massive context</code></pre>

    <h3>Key Architectural Innovations</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grouped Query Attention (GQA):</strong> Reduces memory, maintains quality</li>
      <li><strong>RoPE (Rotary Position Embeddings):</strong> Better position encoding for long context</li>
      <li><strong>SwiGLU Activation:</strong> Replaces GELU for better performance</li>
      <li><strong>RMSNorm:</strong> Simpler, faster than LayerNorm</li>
      <li><strong>Mixture of Experts:</strong> Conditional computation for massive scale</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Training Process</h2>

    <h3>Phase 1: Pre-training</h3>
    
    <p>Unsupervised learning on massive text corpora:</p>

    <pre><code>{`Objective: Next Token Prediction
Given: "The cat sat on the"
Predict: "mat" (or any valid continuation)

Loss = -log P(w_t | w_1, ..., w_{t-1})

Training Data Scale:
- GPT-3: ~500B tokens
- Llama 2: 2T tokens  
- GPT-4: Estimated 10T+ tokens

Training Compute:
- GPT-3: ~3,640 petaflop-days
- PaLM: ~2,500 petaflop-days
- Modern LLMs: 10,000+ petaflop-days`}

Cost: $2M - $100M+ per training run</code></pre>

    <h3>Phase 2: Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)</h3>
    
    <p>Train on high-quality instruction-response pairs:</p>

    <pre><code>{`Example training data:
{
  "instruction": "Explain quantum computing to a 10-year-old",
  "response": "Imagine a magical coin that can be heads AND tails at the same time..."
}

Dataset size: 10K - 1M examples
Duration: Hours to days
Purpose: Align model to follow instructions`}</code></pre>

    <h3>Phase 3: Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)</h3>
    
    <p>Optimize for human preferences:</p>

    <div class="diagram-container">
      <pre class="mermaid" set:html={`graph TB
    A[Generate Multiple Responses] --> B[Human Ranks Responses]
    B --> C[Train Reward Model]
    C --> D[PPO Training]
    D --> E[Improved LLM]
    E --> A

    style A fill:#e1f5ff
    style E fill:#d4edda`} />
    </div>

    <pre><code>Steps:
1. Collect human rankings of model outputs
2. Train reward model to predict human preferences
3. Use PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization) to optimize LLM
4. Balance: maximize reward while staying close to SFT model

Key challenge: Reward hacking (model exploits reward function)</code></pre>

    <h3>Modern Alternatives to RLHF</h3>

    <h4>Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)</h4>

    <p>DPO simplifies RLHF by eliminating the separate reward model, making training more stable and efficient:</p>

    <pre><code>Traditional RLHF:
  SFT Model → Reward Model → RL Training (PPO) → Final Model
  (Complex, unstable, requires careful tuning)

DPO Approach:
  SFT Model → Direct Optimization on Preferences → Final Model
  (Simpler, more stable, competitive performance)

Key Formula:
  L_DPO = -E[log σ(β log π(y_w|x)/π_ref(y_w|x) - β log π(y_l|x)/π_ref(y_l|x))]

  Where:
  - y_w = preferred (winning) response
  - y_l = less preferred (losing) response
  - π_ref = reference model (SFT checkpoint)
  - β = temperature parameter

Advantages:
• No separate reward model needed
• More stable optimization
• Faster training
• Competitive or better results than RLHF
• Widely adopted in open-source models (Mistral, Zephyr)

Used by: Mistral, Zephyr, many Hugging Face models</code></pre>

    <h4>Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback (RLAIF)</h4>

    <p>RLAIF replaces human raters with AI models, enabling massive scale:</p>

    <pre><code>Process:
1. Generate multiple responses from LLM
2. Use stronger AI model (e.g., GPT-4) to rank responses
3. Train using RLHF or DPO on AI-generated preferences

Benefits:
• Dramatically scales feedback collection (1000x+ cheaper)
• Consistent evaluation criteria
• Faster iteration cycles
• Combines human principles (in AI rater) with AI scale

Drawbacks:
• AI rater biases propagate to final model
• May miss nuanced human preferences
• Requires high-quality AI rater

Hybrid Approach (Best Practice):
• Use human feedback for core values and edge cases
• Use AI feedback for bulk preference data
• Validate AI ratings on sample of human-judged data

Used by: Google (Constitutional AI for Bard/Gemini),
         Anthropic (Claude with Constitutional AI)</code></pre>

    <h4>Constitutional AI</h4>

    <p>Anthropic's approach to alignment using AI-generated feedback based on explicit principles:</p>

    <pre><code>Two-Phase Training:

Phase 1: Self-Critique and Revision
1. Model generates initial response
2. Model critiques response against constitutional principles
3. Model revises response to align with principles
4. Supervised learning on revised responses

Phase 2: RLAIF
1. Generate multiple responses to prompts
2. Model evaluates which response better follows principles
3. Train preference model on AI evaluations
4. Use RL to optimize for constitutional alignment

Constitutional Principles (examples):
• "Choose the response that is most helpful, harmless, and honest"
• "Avoid responses that could cause physical, emotional, or financial harm"
• "Respect human autonomy and privacy"

Result: Claude's emphasis on helpfulness, harmlessness, honesty (HHH)</code></pre>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">🔄 Evolution of Alignment Methods (2020-2024)</div>
      <p>The field has rapidly evolved:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>2020-2022:</strong> RLHF with PPO becomes standard (InstructGPT, ChatGPT)</li>
        <li><strong>2023:</strong> DPO emerges as simpler alternative</li>
        <li><strong>2023-2024:</strong> RLAIF scales preference collection</li>
        <li><strong>2024:</strong> Hybrid approaches combine human oversight with AI scale</li>
        <li><strong>Trend:</strong> Moving toward more efficient, scalable alignment without sacrificing quality</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Scaling Laws</h2>

    <h3>Chinchilla Scaling Laws</h3>
    
    <p>Optimal model size vs. training data:</p>

    <pre><code>Key Finding: Most LLMs are undertrained!

For compute budget C:
- Optimal model parameters: N_opt ∝ C^0.5
- Optimal training tokens: D_opt ∝ C^0.5

Example:
- Previous approach: 175B params, 300B tokens (GPT-3)
- Compute-optimal: 70B params, 1.4T tokens (Chinchilla)
- Result: Chinchilla outperforms GPT-3 despite fewer parameters

Implication: Training data is as important as model size</code></pre>

    <h3>Emergent Abilities: Understanding the Debate</h3>

    <p>The concept of "emergent abilities" in LLMs refers to capabilities that appear or dramatically improve at scale.
    However, this topic is actively debated in the research community (2023-2024):</p>

    <h4>Original Claim (Wei et al., 2022)</h4>

    <p>Certain capabilities "emerge" unpredictably at scale—appearing suddenly rather than improving gradually:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Few-shot learning:</strong> Learn new tasks from examples in prompt</li>
      <li><strong>Chain-of-thought reasoning:</strong> Break down complex problems step-by-step</li>
      <li><strong>In-context learning:</strong> Adapt behavior based on prompt context</li>
      <li><strong>Multi-step reasoning:</strong> Solve problems requiring multiple inferential steps</li>
      <li><strong>Instruction following:</strong> Understand and execute complex instructions</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Counter-Argument (Schaeffer et al., 2023: "Mirage of Emergence")</h4>

    <p>The appearance of "emergence" may be an artifact of how we measure, not a genuine phase transition:</p>

    <pre><code>Key Points:
• Non-linear metrics can make smooth improvements appear "emergent"
• Using different metrics shows continuous, predictable scaling
• No evidence of sharp transitions when measured appropriately

Example:
- Metric: Exact match accuracy → Appears "emergent" (0% → 60% suddenly)
- Metric: Token-level accuracy → Shows smooth improvement (gradual 10% → 60%)

Implication: The way we measure determines whether we see "emergence"</code></pre>

    <h4>Current Consensus (2024)</h4>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">🔬 Scientific Understanding (2024)</div>
      <p><strong>What we know for certain:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>✅ <strong>Scaling improves capabilities:</strong> Larger models consistently perform better</li>
        <li>✅ <strong>Qualitative shifts occur:</strong> Models cross thresholds where tasks become solvable</li>
        <li>✅ <strong>Measurement matters:</strong> How we evaluate determines perceived emergence</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What remains debated:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>⚠️ Whether improvements are truly "emergent" (sudden) vs. "continuous" (gradual)</li>
        <li>⚠️ Whether emergence is a property of models or metrics</li>
        <li>⚠️ Predictability of capability thresholds</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Practical takeaway:</strong> Scaling reliably improves LLM performance across tasks, enabling
      new capabilities at larger scales. Whether these improvements are "emergent" or "continuous" is an
      ongoing scientific debate, but the practical outcome is clear: bigger, better-trained models can do more.</p>
    </div>

